\NP-hard in $d$-regular graphs and graphs of max degree $d$. One question of
further potential interest would be to extend this analysis to low-dimensional
grid graphs (i.e. induced subgraphs of a square lattice). It's known that
$[1,1]\hHAM$ is \NP-hard in 2-dimensional grid graphs \cite{gridgraphs}, but not
clear whether one should expect $[1,2]\hHAM$ to be hard as well, for example.
Another problem worth considering would be hardness of approximation --- for
instance, when is it possible to distinguish between $[a,a]\hHAM$ graphs and
graphs that aren't even $[a',a']\hHAM$, for some $a' > a$?

