-at most b times.  The following labeling paragraph proves the executable
-certificate equivalence: edge traversal multiplicities have connected support
-and even incident sums in [2a,2b], and an Euler tour recovers the walk.  At
-a=b=1 the walk is exactly a Hamiltonian cycle, so the cyclic ordering checked
-here is the paper's native witness rather than a surrogate.
 
-Hard and easy regimes.  Theorem 1 says [a,b]-Hamiltonicity on d-regular graphs
-is NP-hard for odd d when b<d; this family fixes d=3 and a=b=1.  Lemma 2 is the
-easy boundary to avoid: when odd d has b>=d, duplicating every edge gives an
-Euler tour, and the subsequent two-factor lemma extends the result.  For
-maximum-degree graphs, Lemma 8 likewise makes [1,d]-Hamiltonicity trivial by
-labeling every edge 2.  The paper gives no polynomial certificate-producing
-algorithm in the selected regime; general certificate search is Hamiltonian
-cycle search.  Theorem 1 is worst-case only, so it does not prove this planted
-distribution hard.  G5, G6, and the external oracle transcript are therefore
