index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f8a19d251e554cbe8ff638d7f3003a3a22e7b959
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+# arXiv:2405.16270 — bounded-degree multiple-Hamiltonicity generator
+
+Status: the generator and all local gates pass; the required oracle hardening is
+**not yet scored** because the configured OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key
+limit exceeded” on every bare, structural-hint, and placebo call. These errors
+are preserved in the transcripts and are not counted as model failures.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | A — structural hardness |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple (a normalized cyclic vertex order) |
+| Intuition | decomposition: a cubic Hamilton cycle is the complement of a perfect matching, with a hidden two-page noncrossing order |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+This module instantiates Definition 1 of Liu, Sheffield, and Westover,
+[“Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16270).
+The solver receives a simple undirected 3-regular graph and must exhibit a
+closed walk visiting every vertex exactly once: the paper's native
+`[a,b]-HAM` object with `a=b=1`. Verification is cheap and exact: check that the
+answer is a normalized permutation of all vertices and that every consecutive
+pair, including the closing pair, is an edge.
+
+Generation is inverse, not search-based. A cycle is sampled first, one decoy
+edge per vertex is added as two noncrossing page matchings, and a uniform random
+vertex relabelling hides every role. The known cycle is carried through that
+relabeling. Under the final relabelling, an individual planted edge and an
+individual decoy edge have the same uniform marginal over unordered vertex
+pairs; all vertices have identical degree.
+
+## Why Track A is claimed
+
+Theorem 1 proves `[a,b]-HAM` NP-hard on odd `d`-regular graphs when `b<d`.
+This family uses `d=3, a=b=1`, exactly inside that regime. It avoids the easy
+side of the same theorem: Lemmas 2–3 make odd-regular instances automatic once
+`b>=d`. It also avoids the linear tree algorithm of Lemma 10, the high-ratio
+maximum-degree algorithms of Lemmas 11–12, and the directed degree-three
+equality algorithm of Lemma 16.
+
+The theorem is worst-case, not an average-case result for this distribution.
+Distributional evidence therefore comes from G5/G6: an exact cubic-graph
+perfect-matching/complement search exhausted 100,000 branches on each of eight
+shipping seeds (800,008 calls, 79.856 seconds total) with no witness. Five other
+construction-aware attacks also failed on all eight seeds. The multi-vendor
+no-tool evidence remains pending because of the credential limit described
+above.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=8, seed=0)`, the rendered graph has vertices `0..7` and
+the following complete unordered edge list:
+
+```text
+2-3 0-1 3-7 1-7 4-5 1-3 5-6 0-4 0-5 4-6 2-7 2-6
+```
+
+The task is to return a JSON list of exactly eight distinct vertices, beginning
+with `0`, whose consecutive pairs and last/first pair are listed edges; the
+second entry must be smaller than the last to fix reversal. The exact output
+contract is:
+
+```text
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON list of
+exactly 8 integers. Example: <answer>[0, 3, 7, 2]</answer>
+The example illustrates syntax only. Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+One answer is `<answer>[0, 1, 7, 3, 2, 6, 5, 4]</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping the last vertex returns
+`(False, "wrong_length:7_expected_8")`. The demo has four normalized valid
+cycles among 2,520 normalized permutations and is genuinely hand-solvable.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | `n` | Candidate-space bits | Intended operations | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 12 | 16 | paper-scale illustration |
+| easy | 120 | 653 | 240 | provisional shipping preset; local gates pass |
+| medium | 132 | 737 | 264 | available escalation |
+| hard | 148 | 851 | 296 | last rung under the 300-operation cap |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is currently `easy`. It must be reconsidered only if a
+completed `harden.py` run solves this rung. Beyond `n=148`, the intended route
+exceeds G9's operation cap, so `escalate()` returns `"cap_bound"`.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset–seed plants verify; JSON round-trip included |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reason codes |
+| G3 | pass | tagged, fenced, prose-surrounded answer round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0 hits / 200,000 normalized random permutations; 51.819 s |
+| G5 | pass | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo exact count 4/2,520; exact baseline 800,008 nodes / 79.856 s |
+| G6 | pass | six attacks, each 0/8 successes |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=240` instance builds and verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance checks, 60/60 transported witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 371 chars, 93 estimated tokens, 120 atoms, 240 intended operations |
+
+The six G6 attacks are label-order guessing, local triangle/four-cycle edge
+scores, constrained greedy walking, 64 randomized restarts, spectral circular
+seriation, and the domain-standard exact perfect-matching/complement brancher.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+No row below is a solve or a model failure: all are infrastructure errors and
+the harness correctly refused to issue a verdict.
+
+| Arm | Preset | Scored solved/attempts | Calls made | Outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| structural hint | easy | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| placebo hint | easy | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is unavailable, not zero: neither arm had
+a scored attempt. After the key is replenished, rerun all three arms and update
+`G9_ORACLE_RESULTS`, `selftest_report.json`, and this table from the transcripts.
+
+## Use
+
+From this directory:
+
+```python
+import gen_2405_16270 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(**g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY], seed=42)
+statement = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit instances with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2405.16270 20
+```
+
+After replenishing `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, regenerate the required evidence:
+
+```bash
+cd results/2405.16270
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2405_16270.py
+```
+
+Run structural and placebo modes in separate scratch directories because the
+harness opens its transcript files with mode `"w"`.
+
+## Caveats
+
+The paper proves worst-case NP-hardness, not hardness of these answer-first
+two-page cubic graphs. A stronger planar-Hamiltonicity implementation, a SAT or
+ILP encoding, or a full planarity-embedding attack was not run; the exact
+matching brancher is bounded at 100,000 nodes per seed. The 0/200,000 guess rate
+is under a uniform prior over normalized vertex permutations and does not model
+a solver that conditions on graph edges. The canonical key is a strong
+distance-profile invariant, not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form.
+Most importantly, no multi-vendor oracle result is currently available: this
+directory is locally verified but must not be submitted as hardened until the
+403 credential failure is resolved.
