<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Brian Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nathan Sheffield
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alek Westover
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the following generalization of the Hamiltonian cycle problem:
Given integers <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> and graph <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, does there exist a closed walk in <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that visits every vertex at least <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times and at most <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> times? Equivalently, does there exist a connected <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2a,2b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2a,2b]</annotation></semantics></math> factor of <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b\cdot G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b\cdot G</annotation></semantics></math> with all degrees even? This problem is <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard for any constants <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the graphs produced by known reductions have maximum degree growing linearly in <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. The case <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b=1</annotation></semantics></math> — i.e. Hamiltonicity — remains <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard even in <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs; a natural question is whether this is true for other <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study which <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> permit polynomial time algorithms and which lead to <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hardness in graphs with constrained degrees. We give tight characterizations for regular graphs and graphs of bounded max-degree, both directed and undirected.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Background and Previous Work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hamiltonian cycle problem asks, given a graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, whether <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a closed walk visiting every vertex exactly once.
A classic result of Garey, Johnson and Tarjan is that Hamiltonicity is <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard even in graphs of max degree 3, and in fact even in 3-regular graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">GJT76</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In 1990, Broersma and Göbel proposed two variants of the Hamiltonian cycle problem: for fixed <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, is there a closed walk that visits each vertex exactly <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times, or at least once and at most <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times, respectively? They showed that for constant <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, both of these problems are <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BG90</a>]</cite>. Jackson and Wormald subsequently proved that both problems remain <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-connected graphs for any constant <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">JW90</a>]</cite>. These proofs extend to give hardness of the following generalization:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefin1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defin">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefin1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefin1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefin1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefin1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For fixed integers <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>, a graph <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is called <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a closed walk on <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that visits each vertex at least <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times and at most <math id="Thmdefin1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> times.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Being <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be considered as a graph factor problem. Letting <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b\cdot G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b\cdot G</annotation></semantics></math> be the multigraph obtained by making <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b</annotation></semantics></math> copies of each edge in <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b\cdot G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b\cdot G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">spanning even <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2a,2b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2a,2b]</annotation></semantics></math> factor</span>; that is, a connected subgraph containing all vertices of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, with every degree even and in <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2a,2b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2a,2b]</annotation></semantics></math>. The equivalence of this formulation follows from taking an Euler tour.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There is a substantial body of combinatorics work seeking sufficient conditions for the existence of graph factors and walks; see Plummer’s survey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Plu07</a>]</cite>, the book by Akiyama and Kano <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK11</a>]</cite>, or the survey by Kouider and Vestergaard which focuses specifically on connected factors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">KV05</a>]</cite>. For example, Gao and Richter showed that any <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-connected planar graph is <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">GR94</a>]</cite>, and Schmid and Schmidt gave a polynomial-time algorithm to find such a walk in which only the 3-separators are visited twice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS18</a>]</cite>. Several variants of the problem have also been consider from an algorithmic complexity standpoint. Ganian et al consider the case where <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is nonconstant in <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard for <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq O(n^{1-\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq O(n^{1-\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, but in <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{RP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖱𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{RP}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq\Omega(n/\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq\Omega(n/\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gan+19</a>]</cite>. Nishiyama et al show that there is a polynomial-time algorithm to determine whether a graph has a walk visiting every vertex an odd number of times, but that the problem becomes <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard when each edge can be traversed at most <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> times <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nis+18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we will study the complexity of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in regular graphs and graphs of constant maximum degree. Prior papers have investigated degree conditions sufficient to guarantee <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> — for instance Kouider and Vestergaard showed that, for <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, any 2-connected graph with minimum degree <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="an/(a+b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">an/(a+b)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">KV04</a>]</cite>, and independent papers by Gao and Richter, Jin and Li respectively showed that any <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-connected graph with maximum degree <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b-2</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">JL04</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kai+07</a>]</cite>. However, to our knowledge we are the first to consider the algorithmic complexity of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in general bounded degree graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results are tight characterizations for the hardness of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in regular graphs, graphs of bounded max-degree, and bounded max-degree digraphs. Interestingly, the thresholds for which <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> lead to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hardness are very different in each of these settings. In regular graphs, we find that hardness is determined by the maximum visit count <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in comparison to the degree <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, where the parity of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is very important:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_reptheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2.2.2 Reductions ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span><span id="Thmreptheoremx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When restricted to <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs, <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard if either
<math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d/2</annotation></semantics></math>, or
<math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, <math id="Thmreptheoremx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In graphs of bounded max degree, we find instead that hardness is determined by the ratio of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in comparison to the max degree <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> is a special case:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_reptheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span><span id="Thmreptheoremx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When restricted to graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard if either
<math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, or
<math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, <math id="Thmreptheoremx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For directed graphs, <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> turns out to always be hard in graphs of maximum degree <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and hard in graphs of maximum degree <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_reptheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><a href="#Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></span><span id="Thmreptheoremx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In directed graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmreptheoremx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard otherwise.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_reptheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.2.2 Max degree 4 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></span><span id="Thmreptheoremx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmreptheoremx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmreptheoremx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="Thmreptheoremx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmreptheoremx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmreptheoremx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in directed graphs of maximum degree 4.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The hardness proofs for maximum degree digraphs easily extend to show hardness in regular digraphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Introducing Depletors</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we introduce the notion of a <span id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">depletor</span>  gadget which will be used in all of our hardness reductions.
We introduce this notion by showing that <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is hard in unrestricted graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Broersma, Göbel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BG90</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmlem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In unrestricted graphs, for any constants <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce from <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the standard Hamiltonian cycle problem). Given a graph <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we build a new graph <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by gluing to each vertex <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math> triangles, as shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In Introducing Depletors ‣ 1.3 Preliminaries ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In any <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle each of these triangles must be visited — this accounts for <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math> of the visits for each of the vertices in <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, so the rest of the walk when restricted to <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> must be a Hamiltonian cycle. For the other direction, note that it is possible to satisfy the visit requirements of the each triangle by stepping up to it, walking back and forth <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math> times, and then stepping back down, contributing only <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> additional visit to the vertex we attached the triangles to. So, if <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian then <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2405.16270v1/ham-triangles_svg-tex.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/105;" width="191" height="105"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Reducing <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[3,3]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,3]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general a depletor gadget is some small graph that we glue to the starting graph <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that forces vertices to be visited many times. In <a href="#Thmlem1" title="Lemma 1 (Broersma, Göbel []). ‣ Introducing Depletors ‣ 1.3 Preliminaries ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, triangles are serving as depletors, since they each contribute a visit to the vertex they’re glued to. In future sections, we will need depletors with stronger properties, such as forcing an attachment edge to be traversed many times.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Introducing Labellings</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle, we can label each edge of the graph <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with the number of times it’s traversed in the cycle. These labels satisfy the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the sum of the labels on the edges adjacent to each vertex is an even number at least <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> and at most <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the subgraph on edges with non-zero labels is connected.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now, given such a labeling, we could create a multigraph by including each edge with multiplicity given by its label. Since all vertices in this multigraph have even degree, we can (efficiently) find an Eulerian cycle in the multigraph, which is then an <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle in the original graph. So, finding a labeling with these properties is equivalent to finding an <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle; this perspective will make the problem easier to reason about.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A Technical Note</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we remark that, throughout this paper, degree bounds will be assumed to
be greater than or equal to <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. All problems we consider are trivial in graphs
of maximum degree <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we will refrain from mentioning this special case.
We will use “maximum degree” to refer to any upper bound on the degrees of a
graph. In particular, a graph with no degree larger than <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a max-degree <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
graph even if it does not have a vertex achieving that degree. In directed
graphs, degree refers to the sum of in-degree and out-degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another piece of notation that is important to note is that we will write <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mod y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>mod</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mod y</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the remainder when <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is divided by <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> (as opposed to the equivalence class <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+y\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+y\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Complexity characterization in <math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Easiness</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be solved in polynomial time for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs, as long as either <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq d</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason for this is simple: the following two lemmas demonstrate that <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> connected <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> for such <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For even <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph is <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d/2,d/2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d/2,d/2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For odd <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph is <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d,d]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d,d]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then the graph is Eulerian — taking an Euler tour will visit every vertex exactly <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/2</annotation></semantics></math> times. If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, we can duplicate all its edges to get a <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular multigraph — an Euler tour in that multigraph visits every vertex exactly <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> times.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we use Tutte’s two-factor theorem to extend <a href="#Thmlem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 2.1 Easiness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to more <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the form of Tutte’s two-factor theorem that we use is:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmfact1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_fact">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmfact1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fact 1</span></span><span id="Thmfact1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmfact1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmfact1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmfact1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="Thmfact1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>-regular multigraph, its edges can be partitioned into <math id="Thmfact1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distinct <math id="Thmfact1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-factors (i.e. <math id="Thmfact1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-regular sub-multigraphs).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using <a href="#Thmfact1" title="Fact 1. ‣ 2.1 Easiness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fact</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Broersma, Göbel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BG90</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmlem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If a graph <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, then it’s also <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b^{\prime},b^{\prime}]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b^{\prime},b^{\prime}]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}&gt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}&gt;b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the multigraph corresponding to a <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> walk on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b</annotation></semantics></math>-regular multigraph, so we can find a multigraph two-factor by Tutte’s two-factor theorem. Duplicating all edges in that two-factor, we have a multigraph corresponding to a <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b+1,b+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b+1,b+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> walk on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Repeating this process gives the desired claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since a <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle is also an <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle for <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, we’ve shown the easiness part of the claim.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Hardness</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>Depletors</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first step in the hardness argument is to construct depletors. We would like a small structure that can always be part of an <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle as long as it’s visited at least once.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will describe such a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletor for even <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with two attachment edges and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Add an edge between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(i-j)\mod 2d\in\{-d/2,\dots,d/2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i-j)\mod 2d\in\{-d/2,\dots,d/2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, cut the edges <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{2d},v_{3d/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{2d},v_{3d/2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{d/2+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{d/2+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and add the edge <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{3d/2},v_{d/2+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{3d/2},v_{d/2+1})</annotation></semantics></math> — note that this is in fact a new edge since</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(3d/2-d/2-1)\bmod 2d\not\in\{-d/2,\dots,d/2\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3d/2-d/2-1)\bmod 2d\not\in\{-d/2,\dots,d/2\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">So, if we add an edge leaving the gadget from each of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction will be <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-regdepletors_svg-tex.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/123;" width="238" height="123" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Labellings corresponding to <math id="S2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b,b]</annotation></semantics></math> walks on 4-regular and 5-regular depletors, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmlem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a valid <math id="Thmlem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> traversal of this even-regularity depletor in which each attachment edge is traversed exactly once.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assign each edge <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i},v_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i},v_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> of the gadget to be traversed <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> times, and assign the edge <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{2d},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{2d},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to be traversed <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math> times; see <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that this gadget required two attachment edges. This is a necessary consequence of the regularity constraint: since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even, there must be an even number of half-edges within the gadget, meaning that the number of edges leaving the gadget must be even.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, we can construct a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletor needing only one
leaving edge. Start with the complete graph <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then delete all
edges <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i},v_{i+(d+1)/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i},v_{i+(d+1)/2})</annotation></semantics></math> except for the edge <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{(d+1)/2+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{(d+1)/2+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
At this point, every vertex has degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, except for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{(d+1)/2+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{(d+1)/2+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which have degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. So, we add a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-th vertex <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, with edges
to every other vertex except <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{(d+1)/2+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{(d+1)/2+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a single edge
leaving the construction — note that the result is now <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmlem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a valid <math id="Thmlem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> traversal of this odd-regularity depletor in which the attachment edge is traversed exactly twice.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assign all the edges in the loop passing through all vertices to be traversed <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math> times, and then add one more traversal to the edges <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i},v_{(i+1)\bmod d+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i},v_{(i+1)\bmod d+2})</annotation></semantics></math>; see <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Reductions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These depletor gadgets let us use the construction from <a href="#Thmlem1" title="Lemma 1 (Broersma, Göbel []). ‣ Introducing Depletors ‣ 1.3 Preliminaries ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to resolve the case of even <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6</span></span><span id="Thmlem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For even <math id="Thmlem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmlem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b&lt;d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b&lt;d/2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmlem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-2b+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-2b+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs, which is known to be <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard as long as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-2b+2)&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-2b+2)&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> — i.e. as long as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-2b+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-2b+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by attaching <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b-1)</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletor of <a href="#Thmlem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to each vertex. Recall that this depletor required two attachment edges, so <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular. But now, note that in the process of visiting each depletor gadget once, each vertex from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> must be visited at least <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math> times, leaving room for only <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> additional visit. So, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> was not Hamiltonian, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>-Hamiltonian for any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> was Hamiltonian, then by <a href="#Thmlem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we can extend the Hamiltonian cycle to a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle by visiting each depletor exactly once.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This argument is not immediately sufficient for the case of odd regularity, however. Applying the same construction would yield the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7</span></span><span id="Thmlem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For odd <math id="Thmlem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmlem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmlem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To be tight with our easiness results, however, we would like to show hardness of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. The gap here is coming from our base case: the hard problem we have to reduce from in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas we need to show that even <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> are hard. Fortunately, a slightly more involved reduction closes this gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8</span></span><span id="Thmlem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For odd <math id="Thmlem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmlem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmlem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph; we construct <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Glue <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-3</annotation></semantics></math> depletors to each vertex in <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Trisect each of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>’s original edges — that is, replace them with paths containing <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate nodes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Glue <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-2</annotation></semantics></math> depletors to each of the intermediate nodes just added.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Using the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletors of <a href="#Thmlem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, this construction is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular. We would like to show that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d-1,d-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d-1,d-1]</annotation></semantics></math> Hamiltonian if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian, and otherwise not even <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d-1]</annotation></semantics></math> Hamiltonian — this suffices to prove the lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2405.16270v1/ham-3regundirred_svg-tex.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/91;" width="215" height="91"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Reduction from <math id="S2.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs to <math id="S2.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[4,4]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[4,4]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs. Red outgoing edges represent depletors.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, note that if you ignore the depletors, each intermediate node must be visited exactly once, since the depletors contribute <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-2</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> allowed visits. Thus, each edge gadget must be labeled either <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,0,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,0,2</annotation></semantics></math>; see <a href="#S2.F4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.2 Reductions ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-regedge_svg-tex.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/112;" width="96" height="112" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The two possible labelings of the edge gadget.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The depletors contribute <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-3</annotation></semantics></math> visits to each of the original vertices, so each has an additional label budget of only <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>; this means that for every original vertex, exactly two of the three incident edge gadgets must be labeled <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,1</annotation></semantics></math>. So, the edges of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,1</annotation></semantics></math> edge gadgets in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> form a 2-factor; see <a href="#S2.F5" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2.2 Reductions ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. But now, suppose this <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-factor had multiple connected components. Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,0,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,0,2</annotation></semantics></math> edges do not create connectivity, this would mean that the labeling of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> was not connected, and so couldn’t correspond to a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> walk. Thus, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> had a connected 2-factor, i.e. a Hamiltonian cycle.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-regundirlabelingnewnew_svg-tex.png" id="S2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/114;" width="143" height="114" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The edge gadgets labeled <math id="S2.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,1</annotation></semantics></math> must correspond to a Hamiltonian path.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The reverse direction follows by the same logic: given a Hamiltonian cycle in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we label all edge gadgets corresponding to the cycle <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,1</annotation></semantics></math> and all edge gadgets not in the cycle <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,0,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,0,2</annotation></semantics></math>; visiting each depletor once gives a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d-1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d-1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> labeling.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the above lemmas gives a complete characterization of the hardness of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in regular graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When restricted to <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs, <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard if either <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d/2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now have a tight understanding of when <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is hard in regular graphs. If, instead, we just cared about graphs with constant maximum degree, the hardness results would translate, but easiness would not. We show in this section that in fact the true threshold for hardness in this setting looks very different: complexity is determined not by <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> but by the ratio <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For our easiness result, we will note that testing <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>-Hamiltonicity is easy in trees, and then show that when <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, any failure of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>-Hamiltonicity is witnessed by a tree-like subgraph. To show hardness, we will exploit a few low-degree vertices to construct and fine-tune highly efficient depletor gadgets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Easiness</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we observe that without loss of generality an <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> walk never traverses a given edge more than <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> times:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmlem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmlem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph. If <math id="Thmlem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="Thmlem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> labeling, it has such a labeling with maximum label at most <math id="Thmlem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take such an <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> walk, and consider an edge with label more than <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of the vertices incident to that edge is therefore visited at least <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+1</annotation></semantics></math> times by the walk — so, if we subtract <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> from the label of that edge, each of them will still be visited at least <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times, and the labeling will still be valid.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we give an algorithm for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in trees, which will be an important component of our algorithm for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in general graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10</span></span><span id="Thmlem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmlem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmlem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree. There is a linear-time algorithm to determine whether <math id="Thmlem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Arbitrarily root the tree. We will work from the bottom-up, recording for each edge the set of label values that can extend to a valid labeling in the subtree below it.
In fact, we will show that for each edge <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> the set of labels for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> that can extend to a valid labelling in the subtree below <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\ell_{e},\ell_{e}+2,\ldots,u_{e}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\ell_{e},\ell_{e}+2,\ldots,u_{e}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> for even integers <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e},u_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e},u_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it will suffice to record for each edge <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> the minimum and maximum labels <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e},u_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e},u_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> which can extend to valid labellings in the subtree below <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Start by setting <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e}=2a,u_{e}=2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e}=2a,u_{e}=2b</annotation></semantics></math> for each edge <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> incident to a leaf of the tree: these edges must be traversed at least <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> times in order to visit the leaves <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times.
Now we repeatedly apply the following process to find <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{e},\ell_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{e},\ell_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> for all edges in the tree:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Find a vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> such that all edges in the subtree rooted at <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> have been labelled.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{v},U_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{v},U_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sum of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e^{\prime}},u_{e^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e^{\prime}},u_{e^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively over edges <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> going down from <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be the edge going up the tree from <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that the valid labels for <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely the even numbers in the interval <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{e},u_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{e},u_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e}=\max(2,2a-U_{v}),\quad u_{e}=2b-L_{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e}=\max(2,2a-U_{v}),\quad u_{e}=2b-L_{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">First we argue that labelling for <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> must lie in this set; then we will argue that any labels in this set can be extending to labellings on the subtree.
The label on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> must be even because all of the edges going down from <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> have even labels (by induction). The label on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be zero, or else the labelling is not connected.
The label on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> must be at least <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a-U_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a-U_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> or else vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not visited enough times.
The label on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be larger than <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b-L_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b-L_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> or else vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is visited too many times.
It is clear that for any even <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[\ell_{e},u_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[\ell_{e},u_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math>, if we give <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> label <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is a compatible labelling of the subtree rooted at <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">If at any step in the process an edge <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is given <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e}&gt;u_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e}&gt;u_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we deduce that there is no valid labelling of the tree.
Otherwise, the process will terminate with every edge having <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e},u_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e},u_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e}\leq u_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e}\leq u_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To finish our check of whether the tree is <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> we simply iterate over all possible ways of assigning labels to the edges incident to the root, taken from their corresponding allowed sets <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{e},u_{e}]\cap 2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{e},u_{e}]\cap 2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there is any combination of labels of the edges incident to the root that satisfies the root, then by construction of the allowed label sets, we can extend the labellings given to each <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> incident to the root to the entire subtree below <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can label the entire tree.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We now use this procedure for trees as part of an algorithm for <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in general graphs in the case <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Afterwards we will analyze the case <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is simple but (somewhat surprisingly) meaningfully different from the case <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmlem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}\geq(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}\geq(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a polynomial-time algorithm to test whether a graph <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of max degree <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first step of the algorithm is to search for </span><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">tree derivations</span><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text">. Call an edge <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> </span><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">dangerous</span><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text">  if it does not belong to any cycle in <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (the set of dangerous edges can be compute easily using connectivity tests on modified versions of the graph). The tree derivation stage will assign labels to all dangerous edges.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The means of performing a derivation is exactly as described in <a href="#Thmlem10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ 3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. The set of dangerous edges forms a forest, so for each tree in the forest we run the algorithm from <a href="#Thmlem10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ 3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> to find an <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> labeling that satisfies all vertices except possibly the root. If any of these tree derivations fail, <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> — the failure of a tree derivation gives a set of vertices that cannot be mutually satisfied under any labeling, since there can’t be any more edges into this set.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-fancyalg.png" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/174;" width="262" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The algorithm performs all tree derivations, then labels disjoint cycles, then merges paths into those cycles, and finally labels all remaining edges with <math id="S3.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">If none of these tree derivations fail, we have a labeling of all dangerous edges that satisfies all vertices not involved in any cycle. Call a vertex </span><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">good</span><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text">  if it’s involved in at least one cycle. The next step of our argument is to show that, no matter what labeling we choose for the dangerous edges, there is always an assignment to the remaining edges that visits each good vertex between <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> times, showing that <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> unless a tree derivation fails.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Existence of a labelling, given that tree derivations succeed</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now give a procedure which finds a labelling of the remainder of the graph once all tree derivations have succeeded; we stress that we don’t actually run this procedure in our algorithm, it is an analysis tool to demonstrate the existence of a labelling.
The procedure is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Find an arbitrary cycle in the graph, and label all of its edges with <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Mark the vertices involved in that cycle <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">happy</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While there remains a cycle involving only unhappy vertices, repeat the above step with that cycle.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, suppose there still exists an unhappy good vertex. Since good vertices are involved in cycles, there must exist a path that starts and ends in happy vertices but otherwise contains only unhappy good vertices. (Here, we’re allowing that “path” to potentially start and end at the same happy vertex — this is a slight abuse of terminology.) Find such a path. If <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is even, label all edges in the path with <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, give the edges in the path alternating labels of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Mark all vertices along the path happy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Repeat the above step until all good vertices are happy. Then, label all unlabeled edges of the graph with <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we analyze the procedure. First, note that all edges are assigned nonzero
weight, so this construction is connected<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              This is the place where the
argument fails for <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In that case, the edges assigned <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math> would be
given zero weight, and so we wouldn’t ensure connectivity.</span></span></span>. Labeling a full
cycle with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>’s increases every vertex label sum by either <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>, and all
other labels used in this construction are even values, so this labeling gives
even sums into every vertex. So, the only point to argue is that every vertex is
given sum between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b</annotation></semantics></math>. This holds for the non-good vertices by virtue
of the tree derivations succeeding. Now, note that for each good vertex, at some
point we label two of its incident edges either <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In either case, this ensures that they have total weight at least <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>. By
<a href="#Thmlem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we can decrease all labels assigned while performing tree
derivations to be at most <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the sum of the labels incident to any
good vertex is at most</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a+(d-2)2a=(d-1)2a\leq 2b." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a+(d-2)2a=(d-1)2a\leq 2b.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final point to note in this section is the following, covering the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> case:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 12</span></span><span id="Thmlem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every connected graph of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Label all edges <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. This visits each vertex at least once, and at most <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> times.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Together, <a href="#Thmlem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#Thmlem12" title="Lemma 12. ‣ Existence of a labelling, given that tree derivations succeed ‣ 3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> give our easiness results. Note that we have easiness for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}\geq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}\geq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, unless <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case we need <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}\geq d</annotation></semantics></math>. The first thing we will observe in the next section is that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard, so this distinction is necessary.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Hardness</h3>

<div id="Thmlem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 13</span></span><span id="Thmlem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmlem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,d-1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, this follows by our hardness results in <a href="#Thmlem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 2.2.2 Reductions ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> — a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph is in particular a graph of maximum degree <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even, we can use the exact same construction as in the proof of that lemma, but just use 3-regular depletors instead of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletors, since we’re no longer concerned with regularity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, though, we will show hardness for much larger values of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> than
are hard in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs. Proving this claim will require a new depletor
construction, which we call a <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">chain depletor</span>, since it’s composed of a
long chain of subunits:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 14</span></span><span id="Thmlem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> be integers with <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b&lt;a(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b&lt;a(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any integer <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k\in[2,2a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k\in[2,2a]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a gadget of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and with a single attachment edge, which can be part of an <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle if the attachment edge is labeled <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>, but cannot be satisfied if the attachment edge is labeled <math id="Thmlem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the gadget shown in <a href="#S3.F7" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> — a <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-edge path on two intermediate vertices, with depletors of total depletion <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> attached to the second intermediate vertex.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-linknew_svg-tex.png" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:105/67;" width="105" height="67" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>A “chainlink” gadget, forcing <math id="S3.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leq x+(2b-2a-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leq x+(2b-2a-D)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Gadgets of this form will serve as the links in the chain. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be the label leaving the first intermediate vertex, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> be the label leaving the second intermediate vertex, and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> be the label on the edge between the two vertices. In order to satisfy the visit counts of the first vertex, we must have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+m\geq 2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+m\geq 2a</annotation></semantics></math>, and in order to satisfy the visit counts of the second vertex we must have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+m+D\leq 2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+m+D\leq 2b</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding these constraints together, we find that if <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;x+(2b-2a-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;x+(2b-2a-D)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no setting of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies both of them. On the other hand, if <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[2,2a-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[2,2a-2]</annotation></semantics></math>, then setting <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2a-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2a-x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x+(2b-2a-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x+(2b-2a-D)</annotation></semantics></math> not only satisfies all visit count constraints, but also preserves connectivity <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">so long as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math></em>. So, this gadget forces its “outgoing” edge to have label at least <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2b-2a-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2b-2a-D)</annotation></semantics></math> more than its “incoming” edge, and is satisfied by an outgoing label exactly that large as long as its incoming edge had label between <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Of course, in constructing this link gadget, we assumed we were able to deplete a vertex by <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. So if we want to use these gadgets for anything, we first need some sort of depletors to start with. Fortunately we do have some depletors: using for instance the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletor of <a href="#Thmlem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2.2.1 Depletors ‣ 2.2 Hardness ‣ 2 Complexity characterization in 𝑑-regular graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (or an edge with a triangle on the end) gives <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> depletion, and gluing a single vertex along an edge gives <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> depletion. The overall goal of the chain depletor construction is to get a single gadget with depletion in between <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>, since for our reductions we will need to fine-tune the depletion exactly.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For our construction, we chain together multiple copies of the link gadget, identifying the outgoing edge of one with the incoming edge of the next, and finally ending in a single vertex, as shown in <a href="#S3.F8" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-chainnew_svg-tex.png" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/63;" width="238" height="63" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>A chain of link gadgets forcing  <math id="S3.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})\geq y\geq 2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})\geq y\geq 2a</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose the link gadgets are constructed with depletions <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dots,D_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dots,D_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> be the labels assigned to the attachment edge and final edge of the chain, respectively. By our analysis of the link gadget, this construction is unsatisfiable if the final edge has label <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;x+\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;x+\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. We would therefore like to choose the depletions <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})=2a-2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}(2b-2a-D_{i})=2a-2k</annotation></semantics></math>, since then if the incoming edge has too small a label <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;2k</annotation></semantics></math>, this will make setting <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 2a</annotation></semantics></math> impossible, and so ensure that the vertex at the end of the chain can’t be satisfied. We also want to make sure that the chain <em id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> satisfiable if the incoming edge is labeled <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=2k</annotation></semantics></math>; to guarantee that, it suffices by our analysis of the link gadgets to ensure that <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a-2]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, since then the construction is satisfied by greedily labeling each edge, from attachment edge to the final edge, with the smallest allowable value.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will now show that, except in the case where <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, which we will handle slightly differently, it is always possible to construct such a sequence of <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\left\lfloor\frac{b}{a}-1\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\left\lfloor\frac{b}{a}-1\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2b\mod 2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2b\mod 2a</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b=2a(M+1)+L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b=2a(M+1)+L</annotation></semantics></math>. We will use three kinds of links, with the following depletions:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{large}}=2a(M+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>large</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{large}}=2a(M+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, created by gluing <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M+1)</annotation></semantics></math> single edges. We have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2b-2a-D_{\text{large}})=L-2a." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>large</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2b-2a-D_{\text{large}})=L-2a.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{medium}}=2aM+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>medium</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{medium}}=2aM+2</annotation></semantics></math>, created by gluing <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> single edges and one 3-regular depletor. We have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2b-2a-D_{\text{medium}})=L-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>medium</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2b-2a-D_{\text{medium}})=L-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{small}}=2aM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{small}}=2aM</annotation></semantics></math>, created by gluing <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> single edges. We have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2b-2a-D_{\text{small}})=L." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>small</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2b-2a-D_{\text{small}})=L.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">To verify that these links are well defined, we must check that the maximum degree in the link is at most <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption.
Thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq d-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq d-3</annotation></semantics></math>, so the degree constraint allows us to glue <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M+1</annotation></semantics></math> depletors to a vertex in our link gadget.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">As long as <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is not a multiple of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is positive, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-2a</annotation></semantics></math> is negative, and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(L,L-2,L-2a)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(L,L-2,L-2a)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Bezout’s lemma, there must
therefore exist non-negative integers <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(L-2a)+w_{2}(L-2)+w_{3}L=2a-2k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(L-2a)+w_{2}(L-2)+w_{3}L=2a-2k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">So, we have constructed a multiset of links with
the desired sum. Now we give an ordering of the links in the multiset such that
such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a-2]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a-2]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct the ordering greedily. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the
multiset of links we found above. While</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\neq 2a," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\neq 2a,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">we choose an element <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2b-2a-D^{*})+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2b-2a-D^{*})+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\in[2,2a],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">set <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j+1}=D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j+1}=D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
remove <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now show that such a <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily exists.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, and adding a large link brings the sum outside the desired interval. That is,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L-2a)+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\leq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L-2a)+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then, since adding all remaining elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> would bring the sum to <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> cannot consist only of large links. <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-2</annotation></semantics></math> are both non-negative, and we know</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\leq 0+2a=2a," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L+2k+\sum_{i=1}^{j}(2b-2a-D_{i})\leq 0+2a=2a,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">so adding either a medium or a small link would be valid. If, on the other hand, adding a large link would be valid, then either <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains a large link and we can add that, or <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains only small and medium links, in which case since <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-2</annotation></semantics></math> are both non-negative we can add them in any order. Repeating this argument on <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> until we reach a sum of <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a</annotation></semantics></math> will give a greedy construction for the link sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have established the lemma in the case that <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\nmid b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\nmid b</annotation></semantics></math>; we now deal with the case where <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so now none of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-2a</annotation></semantics></math> are positive. In this case, we will build our chain using <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{medium}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>medium</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{medium}}</annotation></semantics></math> link, and feed the final edge into a <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular depletor as opposed to a single vertex, as in <a href="#S3.F9" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/ham-chain2new_svg-tex.png" id="S3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/66;" width="262" height="66" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>A chain of link gadgets forcing <math id="S3.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-2(k-1)\geq y\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-2(k-1)\geq y\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the attachment edge is labeled <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>, then the final edge can be labeled at least <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and so this is satisfiable. However if the attachment edge is labeled <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;2k</annotation></semantics></math>, then the final edge can’t have positive label, and so the final depletor is not visited.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we have these fine-tunable depletors, we can glue them to the vertices of a graph to contribute a specific number of artificial visits. However, in order to contribute <em id="S3.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">enough</em> visits in our reduction, it is sometimes necessary to also deplete along the edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 15</span></span><span id="Thmlem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmlem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> be constants with <math id="Thmlem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b&lt;a(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b&lt;a(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any integer <math id="Thmlem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k\in[2,2a-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k\in[2,2a-2]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a gadget of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and with two attachment edges with the following properties:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gadget can be satisfied if both attachment edges are labeled <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gadget cannot be satisfied if an attachment edge is labeled less than <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gadget can be both satisfied and connected if both attachment edges are labeled <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gadget cannot be connected if an attachment edge is labeled less than <math id="S3.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the gadget shown in <a href="#S3.F10" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>: a path on <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate vertices, where the <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> middle vertices each have <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b-2a+2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b-2a+2k</annotation></semantics></math> worth of depletion glued to them — this can be constructed using the chain depletors of <a href="#Thmlem14" title="Lemma 14. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-edgedepletor.png" id="S3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:191/155;" width="191" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>An edge gadget that is satisfiable if both inputs are <math id="S3.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfiable and connected if both inputs are <math id="S3.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">The middle edge can be assigned non-negative label only if both attachment edges are labeled at least <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>, and can be assigned positive label only if both attachment edges are labeled at least <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">We can think of this edge gadget as effectively providing <math id="S3.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> depletion to both sides. So, we can now give a reduction to show our complete complexity characterization:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When restricted to graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard if either
<math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, or
<math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The easiness results were established in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Easiness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, and the case
of <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#Thmlem13" title="Lemma 13. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. So, it suffices to show a reduction from
<math id="S3.SS2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in a <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in a max-degree <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
graph <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;a\leq b&lt;(d-1)a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;a\leq b&lt;(d-1)a</annotation></semantics></math>. By replacing the edges of <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with
the edge gadgets of <a href="#Thmlem15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 3.2 Hardness ‣ 3 Complexity characterization in graphs of max-degree 𝑑 ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, we can introduce up to
<math id="S3.SS2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3(2a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3(2a-1)</annotation></semantics></math> depletion at each vertex, and by gluing up to <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-3)</annotation></semantics></math> additional
chain depletors to each original vertex, we can introduce up to <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a(d-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a(d-3)</annotation></semantics></math>
more. Since</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3(2a-1)+2a(d-3)\geq 2b-2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3(2a-1)+2a(d-3)\geq 2b-2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">and all of these depletors are
fully fine-tunable, we can ensure exactly <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2b-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2b-2</annotation></semantics></math> depletion on each original
vertex of <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that up to but no more than <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> of the edge
gadgets incident to each original vertex can be connected; thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.SS2.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has a connected <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-factor.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Complexity characterization in directed graphs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the case of undirected graphs, where we found that sufficiently large size of the <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> range compared to degree made <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> easy, we will show that in directed graphs every case is hard, except when the <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> range is very small (contains only a single element) and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Just as in the directed case, we can think of the problem of <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> as finding labels. More precisely, a directed graph is <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there is a connected labeling of the edges such that every vertex has in-label sum <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and out-label sum <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the directed case the equivalence of these notions follows from taking an Euler tour on the multigraph generated by viewing the labels as multiplicities for edges.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Easiness</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First we give a simple algorithm for the easy cases of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in directed graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 16</span></span><span id="Thmlem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="Thmlem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a polynomial-time algorithm to decide <math id="Thmlem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in directed graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a directed graph of maximum degree <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
If any vertex has in-degree or out-degree <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> we immediately deduce that the
graph is not <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>; in the remainder of the proof we assume there are
no vertices with in-degree or out-degree <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our algorithm for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> begins as follows: while there exists a vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the graph with only a single unlabeled out-edge, or only a single unlabeled in-edge, assign that edge the unique label that satisfies <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. When that process terminates, each vertex must have either zero or two unlabeled in-edges, and either zero or two unlabeled out-edges. So, if we look at the subgraph on unlabeled edges, and think of them as undirected, we must have a union of even-length cycles. For each of those cycles, assign labels by alternating between <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{a}{2}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{a}{2}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lceil\frac{a}{2}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lceil\frac{a}{2}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, check whether the assigned labels represent an <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> labeling; if so accept, if not reject.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{a}{2}\right\rfloor+\left\lceil\frac{a}{2}\right\rceil=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{a}{2}\right\rfloor+\left\lceil\frac{a}{2}\right\rceil=a</annotation></semantics></math>, if our labeling violates a vertex visit constraint, that constraint must have been violated during the forced initial phase. Similarly, since this process only labels edges with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> during the initial phase, if connectivity fails then no labeling can achieve connectivity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Hardness</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Max degree <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now show hardness of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will argue by induction. The following lemma constitutes the inductive step of the argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 17</span></span><span id="Thmlem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular digraphs. Then, for any <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular digraphs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular digraph. We construct <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing each vertex of
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with a </span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">vertex gadget</span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text">  consisting of three </span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">hub vertices</span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"> 
connected by </span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">bridge gadgets</span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text">, as shown in <a href="#S4.F11" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-vtxgadget.png" id="S4.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/157;" width="286" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>A vertex gadget. Note that the specific structure of the bridge gadgets is just to preserve <math id="S4.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regularity; what’s important is that they are acyclic. The labelings shown here are the two possible <math id="S4.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> traversals — in a similar manner, any way to enter and leave the gadget at most <math id="S4.F11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times can be turned into an <math id="S4.F11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> traversal.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
First we show that if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> must be <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each
of the bridge gadgets must be visited at least <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times, contributing at least
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> of the in-label and out-label of each of the hub vertices. Thus, the edges
entering and leaving the vertex gadget can be traversed at most <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+a-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+a-a</annotation></semantics></math> times.
That is, each vertex gadget is visited at most <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times and at least once in an <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> tour of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If this is possible, then <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> must be <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the other direction, if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can create labelling of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
proving that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
First, copy the labels on the edges in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to the edges that enter and exit the vertex gadgets.
Now, label the edges internal to the vertex gadgets as follows:
start by assigning all edges in the gadget label <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, increase the edges
in the first bridge gadget by one for every traversal of the first edge out of the vertex in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, increase the labels of edges in both the first and second bridge gadgets by one for each traversal of the second edge leaving the vertex in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This labeling visits each interval vertex at least <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times and at most <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+k</annotation></semantics></math> times, as desired. Thus, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, by applying this construction recursively, we can show our full hardness result:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 18</span></span><span id="Thmlem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="Thmlem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular digraphs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will argue by induction on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that for all constants <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1,k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1,k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard.
Using the classic theorem that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-regular digraphs,
by <a href="#Thmlem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> we deduce <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>; this will serve as the base case of our induction.
Now, assume that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard for any constant <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular this means that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1+k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1+k]</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard.
Then, applying <a href="#Thmlem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> we conclude that
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+k+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+k+1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmlem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ 4.1 Easiness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> and <a href="#Thmlem18" title="Lemma 18. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> together establish <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 4.2.1 Max degree 3 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In directed graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}\in{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard otherwise.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Max degree 4</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hardness for max degree <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> digraphs immediately gives that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
hard in max degree <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> digraphs as long as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section, we will
take a different reduction approach to show that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also hard. We
will use the following depletor-like construction, which we call an
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> gadget</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 19</span></span><span id="Thmlem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[1,a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[1,a]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a gadget of max degree <math id="Thmlem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> with one incoming edge and one outgoing edge that can be part of an <math id="Thmlem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> cycle as long as it’s traversed at least <math id="Thmlem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times and at most <math id="Thmlem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, as a base case, consider the construction in <a href="#S4.F12" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2.2 Max degree 4 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. It is clear that this is an at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> gadget.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-atleastonce.png" id="S4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:119/51;" width="119" height="51" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>A gadget that can be traversed between <math id="S4.F12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, suppose we know how to build an at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math> gadget. Then, the construction in <a href="#S4.F13" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2.2 Max degree 4 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> is an at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> gadget.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-atleastk.png" id="S4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/100;" width="215" height="100" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>A gadget that can be traversed between <math id="S4.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By using at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)</annotation></semantics></math> gadgets, we can give our desired reduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 20</span></span><span id="Thmlem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="Thmlem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in directed graphs of maximum degree <math id="Thmlem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in max degree <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> digraphs. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a max degree <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> digraph. We will make two copies, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>, of the vertex set, and add the edge <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{\text{out}},v_{\text{in}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{\text{out}},v_{\text{in}})</annotation></semantics></math> for every edge <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. We will also add the edges <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{\text{in}},v_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{\text{in}},v_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, for every <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we will add an at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)</annotation></semantics></math> gadget from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction is shown in <a href="#S4.F14" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.2.2 Max degree 4 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2405.16270v1/figures/ham-4regreduction.png" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/186;" width="215" height="186" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Reducing <math id="S4.F14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in max degree <math id="S4.F14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> digraphs to <math id="S4.F14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in max degree <math id="S4.F14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> digraphs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The at-least-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)</annotation></semantics></math> gadget consumes all but <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s available out-label, and all but <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s available in-label, so at most one other edge out of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be given nonzero label, and at most one other edge into <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be given nonzero label. Thus, in order to maintain connectivity between all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, the labeling in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must correspond to a Hamiltonian cycle in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This establishes <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.2.2 Max degree 4 ‣ 4.2 Hardness ‣ 4 Complexity characterization in directed graphs ‣ Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{NP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{NP}}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in directed graphs of maximum degree 4.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion and open questions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now have full characterizations of which <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> make <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graphs and graphs of max degree <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. One question of
further potential interest would be to extend this analysis to low-dimensional
grid graphs (i.e. induced subgraphs of a square lattice). It’s known that
<math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖭𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>-hard in 2-dimensional grid graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">IPS82</a>]</cite>, but not
clear whether one should expect <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be hard as well, for example.
Another problem worth considering would be hardness of approximation — for
instance, when is it possible to distinguish between <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs and
graphs that aren’t even <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a^{\prime},a^{\prime}]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧𝖠𝖬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a^{\prime},a^{\prime}]{\mathsf{\text{-}HAM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
