## Problem and trust

This module instantiates Definition 1 of Liu, Sheffield, and Westover,
[“Complexity of Multiple-Hamiltonicity in Graphs of Bounded Degree”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16270).
The solver receives a simple undirected 3-regular graph and must exhibit a closed
walk visiting every vertex exactly once—the paper's native `[1,1]-HAM` witness.
The checker exactly tests normalization, permutation membership, every traversed
edge, and the closing edge; it accepts any valid Hamilton cycle and never reads
the planted answer.

Generation is inverse and costs `O(n)`: sample the cycle first, add one decoy
edge at every vertex using two independently sampled noncrossing page matchings,
then apply a uniform vertex permutation and shuffle the edges. The certificate
is the planted cyclic order carried through that relabelling; it is never found
by solving the generated graph. A planted edge and decoy edge have the same
one-edge marginal after relabelling, and every vertex has degree three.

## Why Track A

Section 2, Theorem 1 proves `[a,b]-HAM` NP-hard on odd `d`-regular graphs when
`b<d`; this family uses `d=3` and `a=b=1`. It avoids the same section's easy
Eulerian regime (`b>=d` for odd regular graphs), Section 3's linear tree
algorithm, its polynomial high-ratio maximum-degree regimes, and Section 4's
polynomial directed degree-three equality case.

That theorem is worst-case, not an average-case theorem for this generator.
Distributional evidence therefore comes from G5/G6. The standard exact attack
branches on the perfect matching complementary to a cubic Hamilton cycle, adds
forced-edge propagation and premature-subtour pruning, and repeats after random
vertex relabellings. At `n=220` it exhausted 120,012 nodes on each of eight seeds
(960,096 total; 194.403 seconds) without a witness. This stronger attack had
solved 7/8 instances at the discarded `n=120` rung, which is why the ladder was
moved upward rather than trusting the original weak brancher.

## Worked demo

`make_instance(n=8, seed=0)` produces the complete edge list:

```text
2-3 0-1 3-7 1-7 4-5 1-3 5-6 0-4 0-5 4-6 2-7 2-6
```

The answer `<answer>[0, 1, 7, 3, 2, 6, 5, 4]</answer>` gives every vertex once,
starts at 0, fixes reversal by putting the smaller neighbour of 0 second, and
uses a listed edge cyclically. `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`;
dropping the last vertex returns `(False, "wrong_length:7_expected_8")`. The demo
has 4 valid normalized cycles among 2,520 normalized permutations and is
hand-solvable.

