/tmp/2405.16270-src.c2cxlz/main.tex:345:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:a=1hard}
/tmp/2405.16270-src.c2cxlz/main.tex:346:    $[1,d-1]\hHAM$ is $\NP$-hard in graphs of maximum degree $d$.
/tmp/2405.16270-src.c2cxlz/main.tex:349:    When $d$ is odd, this follows by our hardness results in \cref{lem:oddreghard} --- a $d$-regular graph is in particular a graph of maximum degree $d$. When $d$ is even, we can use the exact same construction as in the proof of that lemma, but just use 3-regular depletors instead of $d$-regular depletors, since we're no longer concerned with regularity.
